{"objective_id":"QFORM-FIVE-270-REPAIRED-AUDIT-20260925","account":"fleet02","slot":"B06","run_id":"20260925T103039Z-fleet02-B06-repaired-audit-r01","pass_id":"P1-B06-integrated-Fourier-entropy-heavy-row","source_commit":"4e530247ec0ef4101a6e5ecf621bd35a969c42aa","source_blob":"98e45d53d106db9a205051a4dd4917fc39dc2e3a","tex_line":654,"pdf_page":"UNCHECKED_VISUAL","source_text":"The number five is used only in the case without pivots, through Proposition~\\ref{prop:signs} with $m=3$; the diagonal pivot uses four large eigenvalues of $A$ and the pair cases use three. Four eigenvalues without pivots need not give a third large singular value in any off-diagonal block. Let $N>256$, let $w\\in\\R^N$ have all entries equal to $N^{-1/2}$, and let $A$ be the direct sum of two copies of the $(N+1)\\times(N+1)$ matrix $\\begin{pmatrix}0&w^T\\\\w&0\\end{pmatrix}$. Its nonzero eigenvalues are $1,1,-1,-1$, its diagonal vanishes and every principal $2\\times2$ minor has modulus at most $1/N<\\zeta$, so $A$ has no pivot. For every partition $[n]=I\\sqcup J$ the part of each copy in $A_{I,J}$ lies in a single row or a single column, namely that of the centre of the copy, so $A_{I,J}$ has rank at most two and $E_3(A_{I,J})=0$.","status":"PASS","hypotheses":"As stated on this source line and in its containing result.","dependencies":"Containing dependencies independently reconstructed in P1.","derivation_or_objection":"Checked directly in the repaired-source P1 reconstruction; no independent proof defect found.","downstream":"See containing result.","repair_ref":null,"independence_note":"Fresh repaired-hash audit from zero; old-source PASSes not inherited."}
